## Testing some stereotypes

```{r import data }
library(tidyverse)

snore <- read.table(
  "data/snore.txt",
  header = TRUE
)

glimpse(snore)
summary(snore)
```
## The analysis
```{r making factor from each category variable}
snore$snore <- factor(
  snore$snore,
  levels = c("N","O"),
  labels = c("No","Yes")
)

snore$sex <- factor(
  snore$sex,
  levels = c("F","H"),
  labels = c("Female","Male")
)

snore$tabac <- factor(
  snore$tabac,
  levels = c("N","O"),
  labels = c("No","Yes")
)

```

### Are snorers fatter?

```{r correlation between weight and snoring}
#Checking if the series of data are follow the normality hypothesis

shapiro.test(
  snore$weight[snore$snore=="Yes"]
)

shapiro.test(
  snore$weight[snore$snore=="No"]
)

```

**Interpretation**

The Shapiro-Wilk test revealed that weight was not normally distributed in either group (snorers: p = 0.024; non-snorers: p = 0.020).

Therefore, the assumption of normality was not satisfied then a non-parametric test was selected for the comparison.

```{r visualisation of the statistics }
ggplot(
  snore,
  aes(x = snore, y = weight, fill = snore)
) +
  geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.7, outlier.shape = NA) +
  geom_jitter(
    width = 0.15,
    size = 2,
    alpha = 0.7,
    color = "black"
  ) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c(
      "No" = "steelblue",
      "Yes" = "tomato"
    )
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Body weight of snorers and non-snorers",
    x = "Snoring status",
    y = "Weight (kg)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    legend.position = "none"
  )

```
**Discussions : ** The boxplot compares body weight between snorers and non-snorers. Snorers appear to have a slightly higher median body weight than non-snorers. However, the distributions overlap considerably, suggesting that any difference may be modest. The variability of body weight is similar in both groups, and several observations are present outside the central range of the data. A statistical test is therefore required to determine whether the observed difference is statistically significant.
```{r performing a non-parametric test : wilcox-test}
wilcox.test(weight ~ snore, data = snore)
```

```{r show the mean and median of weight in snorer&non snorer}
# compare the values between non-snorer & snorer in table
snore %>%
  group_by(snore) %>%
  summarise(
    mean_weight = mean(weight),
    median_weight = median(weight),
    sd_weight = sd(weight)
  )
```

**Discussion**

To determine whether snorers were heavier than non-snorers, the normality of weight distributions was assessed using the Shapiro-Wilk test. Since normality was rejected in both groups (p \< 0.05), a Mann-Whitney-Wilcoxon test was performed.

The analysis revealed no significant difference in body weight between snorers and non-snorers (W = 1154, p = 0.908). The graph coincide with previous results.

### Do snorers drink or smoke more

```{r  graph,normality test, parametric test}
ggplot(
  snore,
  aes(x = snore, y = alcool, fill = snore)
) +
  geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.7) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.1) +
  theme_minimal()
#
shapiro.test(
  snore$alcool[snore$snore=="Yes"]
)

shapiro.test(
  snore$alcool[snore$snore=="No"]
)
```

The Shapiro-Wilk test showed that alcohol consumption was not normally distributed among either snorers (p = 0.005) or non-snorers (p \< 0.001). Therefore, a non-parametric Mann-Whitney-Wilcoxon test was used for further analysis.

And i chose to plot the distribution of alcohol consumption between snorers and non-snorers using a boxplot with jittered points to visualize the data.

```{r Wilcox-test of alcool & snoring}
wilcox.test(
  alcool ~ snore,
  data = snore
)
```

The test revealed a significant difference in alcohol consumption between snorers and non-snorers (W = 788, p = 0.009).

Descriptive statistics showed that snorers consumed more alcohol on average than non-snorers (mean = 4.03 vs 2.37). The median alcohol consumption was also higher among snorers (4 vs 0).

These results suggest that snorers consume significantly more alcohol than non-snorers.

```{r compiling alcool & tabac in non-parametric test : Chi² test}
#See the value in a table
table(snore$snore, snore$tabac)
#Seeing correlation between 
chisq.test(
  table(snore$snore, snore$tabac)
)
```

To investigate whether smoking was associated with snoring, a Chi-square test of independence was performed.

The analysis revealed no significant association between smoking status and snoring (χ²(1) = 0.689, p = 0.407).

Among non-snorers, 67.7% were smokers, whereas 57.1% of snorers were smokers. Although these proportions differed slightly, the difference was not statistically significant.

Therefore, smoking status does not appear to be associated with snoring in the present sample.

Overall ,alcohol consumption and smoking status were investigated as potential factors associated with snoring.

A Mann-Whitney-Wilcoxon test revealed that snorers consumed significantly more alcohol than non-snorers (W = 788, p = 0.009). Snorers showed both a higher mean alcohol consumption (4.03 vs 2.37) and a higher median alcohol consumption (4 vs 0).

In contrast, a Chi-square test indicated no significant association between smoking status and snoring (χ²(1) = 0.689, p = 0.407).

These findings suggest that alcohol consumption is associated with snoring, whereas smoking does not appear to be associated with snoring in this sample.

Limit of the study : Exclusion of medical pathology among the population.
